      "path": "calc.py",
      "content": "Two distinct security issues here:\n\n1. **Hardcoded credential**: The string `\"hunter2\"` is a plaintext password committed directly in source code. Secrets should never be stored in source \u2014 use environment variables or a secrets manager (e.g., `os.environ[\"PASSWORD\"]` or a vault integration).\n\n2. **`eval` on a value named `password`**: Even though `password` is currently a local constant, calling `eval()` on it is dangerous. If this variable ever comes from external input, it becomes arbitrary code execution. Additionally, `eval(\"hunter2\")` will raise a `NameError` at runtime because `hunter2` is not a defined Python name \u2014 so the function is also broken as written. Remove `eval` entirely; if you need to return the string, return it directly.",
